  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through next Sunday. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nDry weather looks to persist through the end of this weekend. \nOver the past 24 hours, models have remained very unimpressive \nwith rain totals for the Thursday night to Friday AM cold \nfront, and backed off on any rain chances for Saturday \nnight into Sunday. The next cold front, expected to move \nthrough Thursday night into Friday morning, will be much like \nits predecessors: generally dry, with any localized rain \namounts being less than 0.10 inch.\n\nQuiet, dry weather with temperatures fairly close to average are\nin store for the weekend. As said before, any rain chances\nshould hold off until Sunday night at the earliest. Models are\nin pretty good agreement on dry weather through Sunday, but\ndisagree significantly after Sunday on when/if any rain comes.\nEnsembles are showing a good likelihood a storm system will be\nnear the region with significant precipitation, however exact\nplacement will determine if any rain occurs in the CWA.\n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal with \nNorthern Maine. Overall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index\nblend keeps the Northern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to \nSevere Dryness category with portions of the Upper St. John \nBasin now dipping into the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 25 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake is at Record Low (deep) \nconditions on record (35 year POR). Millinocket, Kenduskeag, \nHadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much Below Normal\nconditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal conditions \napproaching slipping down into Below Normal conditions. \nTherefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the entire CWA in at\nleast Moderate Drought with 58% of the CWA in Severe Drought, \nthis has the potential to worsen with continued precipitation \ndeficits. See the latest NWS Caribou Drought Information\nStatement for more drought details.",
